---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
// The strengths table first (Eron, 2026-10-18: the chart emphasizes Turf's strengths), then the
// honest three-way matrix. Sources, in order: turf-engine's refusal list (engine/tier2.go,
// refusedConstructs), the deployment and authoring skills' references as the 1.2 server ships
// them (divergences, deferrals, imports, workspaces, lifecycle, actions, variables, providers,
// data sources), doof's language-feature-conformance.md (the 2026-10-08 "v1" entry, the
// 2026-10-24 prevent_destroy entry and the 2026-10-27 ephemeral-outputs entry), the rc.1
// acceptance pass, and Restate's own docs for the engine rows. Baselines re-grounded 2026-10-18:
// Terraform 1.16.x and OpenTofu 1.13.x. Turf column moved to 1.1 on 2026-10-26, to 1.2 for its
// release. See "Content provenance" in CLAUDE.md.
const title = 'Turf — Terraform and OpenTofu compatibility, and beyond';
const description =
  'Turf 1.2 against Terraform 1.16 and OpenTofu 1.13: durable runs, encryption at rest, OpenTelemetry, actions, multi-round convergence, and an honest matrix.';
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="subhero"><div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Compatibility · Terraform 1.16 · OpenTofu 1.13 · Turf 1.2</p>
    <h1>Terraform-compatible. And ahead of it.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Turf™ runs the Terraform® language on the OpenTofu™ framework — the whole expression
    language, the function library, registry modules and the provider ecosystem, unchanged. Under it is
    an engine Terraform and OpenTofu don’t have. Where a construct isn’t supported, Turf
    <strong>refuses loudly — it never silently mis-plans</strong> — and says so here.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="wrap">
    <h2>Where Turf is ahead.</h2>
    <p class="prose">Seven things the engine does that neither Terraform nor OpenTofu does, or does only in
    part.</p>

    <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix">
      <caption>Beyond Terraform and OpenTofu</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th class="c">Terraform 1.16</th><th class="c">OpenTofu 1.13</th><th class="c">Turf 1.2</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Durable runs</th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">A convergence lives in the engine, on <a href="https://restate.dev">Restate</a>: every step is journaled as it happens, an interrupted run resumes where it stopped, a plan waiting for approval holds no process, and a rollout outlives the conversation that started it, the server and a dropped client. <a href="/engine/#engine">The engine →</a> <span class="pill ok">ahead</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Encryption at rest</th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">OpenTofu state encryption, with a passphrase or a key provider; migrating an existing state is a no-op run, and <code>enforced</code> refuses plaintext. It protects the statefile the backend writes; the engine’s own store sits under the disk encryption it runs on. Terraform has no state encryption.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>OpenTelemetry built in</th><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Every run is traced as it happens — a span for every call and every attempt, exported over OTLP to Jaeger or any collector — and metrics come out in Prometheus format. Terraform and OpenTofu trace the CLI process, as an experiment; the engine traces the run. <span class="pill ok">ahead</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Provider calls that scale out</th><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Provider operations run in parallel across the engine until the cloud API, not the tool, is the ceiling: per-resource state instead of one statefile and one lock, on a laptop or as a service in the cluster. Terraform and OpenTofu run one process with a <code>-parallelism</code> knob. <span class="pill ok">ahead</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Terraform actions, with overlays</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The Terraform 1.16 action model: six lifecycle events, <code>on_failure</code> halt, continue or taint, a plan-time <code>condition</code>, <code>caller</code>, and <code>invoke</code> for an ad-hoc run. Plus Turf’s overlays: a <code>*.turf.hcl</code> file adds an <code>action_trigger</code> with a <code>target</code> into any module, per environment or in memory for one run. OpenTofu has no action blocks. <span class="pill ok">ahead of OpenTofu</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Turf actions</th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">A <code>turf_action</code> is a step the configuration asks the agent to carry out during the apply — a check, a notification, a question for the user. The apply stops for it, the agent answers with its own tools, and a decision stays the user’s. Any MCP client; no provider to install. <span class="pill ok">ahead</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Multi-round convergence</th><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">One <code>up</code> plans what it can, defers what a round can’t know yet — an unknown count, a provider configured from an endpoint that doesn’t exist yet, a custom resource ahead of its CRD — applies, and plans again until nothing is deferred. A run that can’t progress ends <code>stalled</code> and says why. Terraform’s deferral is an experimental flag and the substrate for Stacks; OpenTofu’s is provider-driven. <a href="/demo/">Watch a stack converge →</a> <span class="pill ok">ahead</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <p class="prose">The engine runs on Restate — in Restate’s words, “a lightweight runtime to turn AI agents,
    workflows, and backend services into durable processes.” Restate keeps a journal of every step, so a
    run resumes from exactly where it left off. <a href="/engine/#engine">How the engine is built →</a></p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section alt"><div class="wrap">
    <h2>Radically honest: the three-way matrix.</h2>
    <p class="prose">Every construct, scored against Terraform 1.16 and OpenTofu 1.13. A refusal names the
    construct and its remedy; nothing is skipped and planned past.</p>

    <!-- Legend -->
    <div class="legend">
      <span class="li"><span class="sym s-yes">✅</span> Supported</span>
      <span class="li"><span class="sym s-part">◑</span> Partial — see the note</span>
      <span class="li"><span class="sym s-no">⚠️</span> Parsed, but diverges</span>
      <span class="li"><span class="sym s-no">⛔</span> Refused by name — never mis-plans</span>
      <span class="li"><span class="sym s-no">➖</span> Not available · coming soon where the note says so</span>
    </div>

    <!-- 2.1 Top-level blocks -->
    <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix">
      <caption>Top-level blocks</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th class="c">Terraform 1.16</th><th class="c">OpenTofu 1.13</th><th class="c">Turf 1.2</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th><code>resource</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Managed resources at every module depth, with <code>count</code> and <code>for_each</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>data</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Read once per plan, in dependency order. A read whose query is unknown, or whose <code>depends_on</code> target has a pending change, moves to the apply, as in Terraform. The value the plan read is the value the apply commits, so what you approved is what is stored.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>module</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Registry, git and local sources, installed by <code>init</code> and locked. A call is held to the child’s declarations before the walk: a missing required input and an undeclared argument are refused together, so a typo appears beside the input it failed to set. A local source may sit outside the configuration directory, so sibling roots can share their modules: every relative path resolves from the configuration directory, as after <code>tofu -chdir</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>variable</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The full ladder: declaration defaults, then <code>TF_VAR_</code>, then <code>terraform.tfvars</code> and <code>*.auto.tfvars(.json)</code>, then <code>var_file</code> in the order listed, then <code>var</code>. Types convert, <code>nullable</code> applies, and <code>validation</code> blocks run at plan; a rule decidable only after an apply is decided then. Values ride the convergence and are never stored.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>output</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Outputs at every depth, with <code>depends_on</code>; a sensitive value must be declared <code>sensitive</code>. A child module’s output may be declared <code>ephemeral</code> and carries its secret into the caller, to a write-only argument or a provider block; a root output may not, as upstream rules. Not yet: a destroy through a provider block that reads one, and a root output over <code>ephemeralasnull()</code> of one. Refused: an output <code>precondition</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>locals</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Fully honored.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>provider</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The configuration is the only source of provider configuration: each <code>provider</code> block is evaluated at every plan, in dependency order, against state and the plan so far. A block reading a value not yet applied configures the provider next round. Root module only: a provider block in a child module is refused (the legacy shape; the module declares <code>configuration_aliases</code> and the call passes <code>providers</code>).</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>terraform &#123;&#125;</code> settings</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>required_providers</code> constraints are enforced and <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code> is honored, checksums verified; a lock change makes a paused plan stale.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>terraform.required_version</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⚠️</td><td class="c s-no">⚠️➖</td><td class="note-cell">Parsed, not enforced. Coming soon. (OpenTofu checks it in <code>.tofu</code> files only.)</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>backend</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The declared <code>backend</code> block is what a workspace opens; <code>backend_config</code> supplies only what the block leaves out, per key — the <code>tofu init -backend-config</code> pattern — and <code>reconfigure</code> moves a store deliberately. The <code>remote</code> backend works for state storage and locking, with the workspace’s execution mode set to local. Encryption: see above.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>cloud</code> block</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Refused. Use <code>backend "remote"</code> against a workspace whose execution mode is local.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>provisioner</code> / <code>connection</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Refused by name, inside <code>removed</code> blocks too: there is no provisioner runtime, so nothing runs silently. Use a provider action bound to the event, or a turf action.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>moved</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Runs against state before the plan, so a rename plans as a no-op rather than a create plus an orphan destroy; chains collapse, a module rename carries everything inside it, and the relocation commits only when the round applies. An import into an address a move vacates the same round is refused: apply the move first.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>import</code> block</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Adopted at plan: the entry reads as a no-op or an update against the real object, and you review the diff before anything is committed; <code>for_each</code>, computed keys and nested-module targets resolve. Root module only (Terraform 1.16 allows the block inside modules). An <code>id</code>, key or <code>for_each</code> unknown at plan defers the import to a later round, where Terraform refuses. Turf generates no configuration: the agent writes the block.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>removed</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>lifecycle &#123; destroy = false &#125;</code> forgets the object and leaves it running. Provisioners inside the block are refused.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>check</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Refused by name rather than planned past. Coming soon; meanwhile an <code>after_create</code> or <code>after_update</code> turf action verifies the same thing, or a policy check runs on the plan.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>action</code> + <code>action_trigger</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The Terraform 1.16 action model, carried by Turf. OpenTofu has no action blocks. <span class="pill ok">ahead of OpenTofu</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>*.turf.hcl</code> overlays</th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Turf’s extension: <code>action</code> blocks and top-level <code>action_trigger</code> blocks with a <code>target</code>, laid over the configuration at every plan. tofu and terraform ignore the file by its name.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>ephemeral</code> resource</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">A value a provider produces that participates in evaluation and never reaches state: open, renew and close all fire, and an exported plan withholds it. One instance each: <code>count</code>, <code>for_each</code> and conditions on an ephemeral resource are refused.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>write-only arguments</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The one channel an ephemeral value has into a managed resource: admitted at a write-only path, refused everywhere else, null on both sides of every plan; a provider that echoes a value back is refused. Rotating a secret reads as a replacement where the provider declares it so.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>List resources / <code>list</code> block / <code>query</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="note-cell">The Terraform 1.14 query paradigm for bulk discovery and import. Coming soon; OpenTofu doesn’t have it either.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>resource identity (provider-declared)</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Read on plan, apply and import and stored in the state record, so a state carrying identity round-trips unchanged and what Turf writes is a plain OpenTofu state.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>identity-based import</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>identity = &#123; … &#125;</code> in an <code>import</code> block or the <code>import</code> command, validated against the provider’s identity schema before the call.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <!-- 2.2 Meta-arguments -->
    <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix">
      <caption>Meta-arguments</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Meta-argument</th><th class="c">Terraform 1.16</th><th class="c">OpenTofu 1.13</th><th class="c">Turf 1.2</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th><code>count</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Resources, data sources and module calls. An unknown count defers the instances to a later round instead of failing, and adding or removing <code>count</code> on an existing object relocates it without a <code>moved</code> block.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>for_each</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Same as <code>count</code>. Cardinality is OpenTofu’s own evaluation: <code>toset([])</code> is zero instances, a map with unknown values still enumerates, and a set with an unknown element defers.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>enabled</code></th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Refused by name on module calls, resources and data sources. Coming soon; meanwhile <code>count = var.enabled ? 1 : 0</code>. (OpenTofu 1.11; absent from Terraform.)</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>depends_on</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Modules, resources and data sources; a data source whose target has a pending change reads at apply, Terraform’s rule. Outputs too.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>lifecycle.create_before_destroy</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Dependents see the new object as soon as its create applies; the old one is deposed and deleted after.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>lifecycle.prevent_destroy</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Honored as OpenTofu 1.12 honors it: a plan that would delete or replace the object fails naming it, in <code>up</code> and in a destroy, and nothing is applied. An expression is accepted, as in OpenTofu (Terraform takes only a literal), so <code>prevent_destroy = var.protect</code> guards while true. <code>destroy = false</code> overrides it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>lifecycle.ignore_changes</code> (incl. <code>["all"]</code>)</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Honored in full, at plan and at apply.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>lifecycle.replace_triggered_by</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">A whole-resource reference fires on anything that leaves a different object behind, an attribute reference on a value that moves. It is also how a replaced host takes its contents with it: the contents’ delete runs this round through the old host, and their create defers to the next round against the new one.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>lifecycle.precondition</code> / <code>postcondition</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">On resources and data sources. Refused on ephemeral resources and on outputs.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>lifecycle.action_trigger</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Six events, <code>on_failure</code> halt, continue or taint (taint on <code>after_create</code> only, as in Terraform), a plan-time <code>condition</code>, and keyed entries per instance. Declarable in the resource, or as a top-level trigger in an overlay. <span class="pill ok">ahead of OpenTofu</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>provider</code> / <code>providers = &#123;&#125;</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Resource <code>provider =</code> and module <code>providers = &#123;&#125;</code>, validated against <code>configuration_aliases</code>; a child module may use different local names than its caller. Provider instance keys are refused.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>timeouts &#123;&#125;</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Decoded against the provider’s schema and sent with the request, as any nested block is; the provider enforces its own deadlines.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <!-- 2.3 Expressions & functions -->
    <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix">
      <caption>Expressions &amp; functions</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th class="c">Terraform 1.16</th><th class="c">OpenTofu 1.13</th><th class="c">Turf 1.2</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>for-expressions, conditionals, splat, <code>dynamic</code> blocks, string templates, full builtin function library</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The whole expression language and builtin function library, inherited from the OpenTofu framework — no function allow or deny list.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>provider-defined functions (<code>provider::…</code>)</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Refused: not wired yet. Coming soon.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>path.module</code> / <code>path.root</code> / <code>path.cwd</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>path.module</code> and <code>path.root</code> are spelled as OpenTofu spells them, relative to the root module. <code>path.cwd</code> is the configuration directory, the <code>-chdir</code> model: an MCP client’s own directory is not something the protocol tells a server.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>terraform.workspace</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The workspace the plan runs against, carried into every child module.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>caller</code> (in action configs)</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The triggering resource: its planned value before a create or update, its applied value after, its prior value on destroy events, where the whole config freezes at plan. <span class="pill ok">ahead of OpenTofu</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <!-- 2.4 Plan / apply options & workflow -->
    <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix">
      <caption>Plan / apply options &amp; workflow</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th class="c">Terraform 1.16</th><th class="c">OpenTofu 1.13</th><th class="c">Turf 1.2</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th><code>-target</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Not offered: every plan is the whole configuration, so no partial apply hides a dependency. To leave something out of a run, the configuration stops declaring it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-exclude</code></th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Same as <code>-target</code>. (An OpenTofu-only flag; Terraform has no <code>-exclude</code>.)</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-refresh-only</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The <code>refresh</code> command: a one-round refresh-only convergence, approved like any plan.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-refresh=false</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>refresh: false</code> skips refreshing managed objects; data sources are read at every plan regardless.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-var-file</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Applied in the order listed and parsed once when the plan starts; a file that fails to parse refuses the call.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-var</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">The top of the ladder (see <code>variable</code>). <code>var</code> always wins over <code>var_file</code>: no interleaving.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-replace</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>replace</code> on the plan command, or <code>taint</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-auto-approve</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>auto_approve</code> runs every round without pausing; turf actions still stop it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>destroy</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Plans everything in state, dependents first, and leaves the files untouched. A <code>before_destroy</code> action only warns here, so the approval is the guard; <code>prevent_destroy</code> refuses the plan.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>import</code> (command)</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">An import-only round: reads the targets and nothing else, stops at its approval point, and the next <code>up</code> shows the diff.</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>-invoke</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>invoke</code> runs one action now: one the configuration declares, or an ad-hoc action laid over it in memory. <span class="pill ok">ahead of OpenTofu</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>taint</code> / <code>untaint</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">Immediate state edits; the next <code>up</code> replaces the instance.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>saved planfile round-trip</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="note-cell">The approved plan is OpenTofu’s json-format plan, served as a resource and consumable by OPA, Checkov and terraform-docs as it is. A <code>.tfplan</code> file round-trip is coming soon.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>provider <code>for_each</code> (multi-instance providers)</th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Refused: one aliased <code>provider</code> block per instance. Coming soon. (OpenTofu-only; Terraform has no provider <code>for_each</code>.)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>state / plan encryption</th><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">See <em>Where Turf is ahead</em>. (An OpenTofu-only feature.)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>early variable evaluation</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell"><code>var</code> in a module <code>source</code> or a <code>backend</code> body resolves from the real root variable values, as <code>tofu init</code> resolves them; a missing value is a diagnostic naming the variable. (Terraform since 1.15.)</td></tr>
        <tr><th><code>.tftest.hcl</code> / <code>.tofutest.hcl</code> test runner</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">⛔</td><td class="note-cell">Test files in the configuration directory are refused. Keep them beside it and run them with <code>tofu test</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>deferred changes / multi-round convergence</th><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="note-cell">See <em>Where Turf is ahead</em>. <span class="pill ok">ahead</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <!-- 2.5 HCL Stacks -->
    <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix">
      <caption>HCL Stacks (components &amp; deployments)</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Construct / capability</th><th class="c">Terraform 1.16</th><th class="c">OpenTofu 1.13</th><th class="c">Turf 1.2</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Component config — <code>.tfcomponent.hcl</code>: <code>component</code> (wraps a module) plus stack-level <code>variable</code> / <code>output</code> / <code>provider</code> / <code>removed</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="note-cell">Not parsed — Turf has no <code>component</code> block. Stacks is Terraform-only; OpenTofu has none of it. Turf’s multi-environment story is below.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deployment config — <code>.tfdeploy.hcl</code>: <code>deployment</code>, <code>orchestrate</code>, <code>identity_token</code>, <code>publish_output</code> / <code>upstream_input</code>, <code>store</code></th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="note-cell">Not parsed — no <code>deployment</code> / <code>orchestrate</code> blocks. Terraform-only.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Capability: multi-environment, multi-round deployment</th><td class="c s-yes">✅</td><td class="c s-no">➖</td><td class="c s-part">◑</td><td class="note-cell">A different model, not the Stacks language: one configuration, a workspace per environment (<code>workspace_name</code> selects the state slot, a <code>var_file</code> carries the environment’s values), promoted by bumping one variable and planning each environment in turn, with an overlay that adds a production-only approval step. Honest caveat: not a single object that rolls out across deployments as a unit, and no <code>orchestrate</code>, <code>identity_token</code> or cross-stack wiring. <a href="https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-examples/tree/main/terraform/language/environments">The example →</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </div></section>

